{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-94125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$\n- $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}$\n- $R(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$\n\n- $Q(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is *not* diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(x)$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b6f09b463c02a4d9a4b7377799c904ba361482b06139c09001341d7c2129713f"} {"question" "q-94125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$P(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$R(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Q(x)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}$\n- $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\n- $R(x)=2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $Q(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is not diagonal.\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P(x)$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b6f09b463c02a4d9a4b7377799c904ba361482b06139c09001341d7c2129713f"} {"question" "q-94132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$P(x)$ and $R(x)$ only"] ["text" "$P(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$R(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$P(x)$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\n- $Q(x)={x}_{3}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2}$\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{3}-3{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is not diagonal.\n\n- $Q(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term ${x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(x)$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b6f09b463c02a4d9a4b7377799c904ba361482b06139c09001341d7c2129713f"} {"question" "q-94135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$P(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$R(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Q(x)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-140982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $Q(x)=({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}$\n- $R(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-140982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $Q(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since \n\n$$\nQ(x)=({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\n So,  it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P(x)$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-140982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b6f09b463c02a4d9a4b7377799c904ba361482b06139c09001341d7c2129713f"} {"question" "q-140982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-140982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$P(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$R(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-140982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$P(x)$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $Q(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term ${x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b6f09b463c02a4d9a4b7377799c904ba361482b06139c09001341d7c2129713f"} {"question" "q-94127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only"] ["text" "$P(x)$ and $R(x)$ only"] ["text" "$P(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only"] ["text" "$Q(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+38{x}_{2}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+6{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+38{x}_{2}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(2{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2} & =2([{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}]-16{x}_{2}^{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})^{2}-16{x}_{2}^{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})^{2}-16{x}_{2}^{2})+38{x}_{2}^{2} & =2({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})^{2}-32{x}_{2}^{2}+38{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n2{y}_{1}^{2}+6{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $2\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b6f09b463c02a4d9a4b7377799c904ba361482b06139c09001341d7c2129713f"} {"question" "q-94149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}$"] ["text" "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}$"] ["text" "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$"] ["text" "$4$ and ${x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}$"] ["text" "$4$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$2$ and ${x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/state.json" "file_sha256" "16d7a5a136fc6fafd30faf10a94b7a24f4dbc353af173196a04d953b37df0e66"} {"question" "q-94142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(3{x}_{1}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3([{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}]-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{ali